{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 42, :comments ["<b>תושב (ezraḥ)</b> — \"הָאֶזְרָח\" (the native): to exclude women, so that one should not learn from the fifteen [commandments] of the Festival of Unleavened Bread to obligate them [women] in sukka just as in matza. \"בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל\" (among Israel): to include converts — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 23:42). And in Torat Kohanim (chapter 17, halakha 9): \"אֶזְרָח\" — this is a native; \"הָאֶזְרָח\" — to exclude women; \"כָּל הָאֶזְרָח\" — to include converts and freed slaves. And the plain sense of Scripture, by way of received tradition: because Scripture said, \"One statute shall there be for you, for the convert and for the native of the land\" (Numbers 9:14), and it said, \"As you are, so shall the convert be before YHWH\" (Numbers 15:15), it is not necessary to repeat and mention in every place both 'the native' and 'the convert'; rather, it says \"every native among Israel,\" meaning everyone who is of Israel, from their greatest to their smallest — so that it should not suffice for one member of the household to dwell in the sukka while the rest sit in the houses, but all of them shall dwell in sukkot. And it is possible that it means: everyone who is like a fresh-flourishing native in his house — to exclude seafarers and those who travel on the road."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The phrase הָאֶזְרָח in the first line refers to the definite article prefixed to the lemma in the verse ('the native'); Ramban's gloss preserves this grammatical observation. The phrase מִגְּדוֹלָם וְעַד קְטַנָּם is rendered literally 'from their greatest to their smallest.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:58:54.119878875Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}